//********************* code execution in js *********************************

Step 1: JavaScript runs inside an environment

JavaScript does not run by itself — it needs an environment to execute, such as:

 Browser (like Chrome, Edge, Firefox) → using the JavaScript Engine inside it.

 Node.js → runs JavaScript outside browsers (for backend apps).

Example engines:

Chrome → V8

Firefox → SpiderMonkey

Edge → Chakra

 Step 2: JavaScript Engine Components

A JavaScript engine (like V8) mainly has two parts:

Memory Heap

Where variables and objects are stored in memory.

Example:

let name = "Samad"; // stored in memory heap


Call Stack

Where the code executes line by line.

Each function call is pushed to the stack when called, and popped out when finished.

🧩 Step 3: Code Execution Process

Let’s say you write this:

function greet() {
  console.log("Hello Samad");
}
greet();


Execution steps:

Global Execution Context (GEC) is created.

The engine creates this context automatically before running any code.

It has two phases:

Creation Phase:
All variables and functions are stored in memory.
(Functions are stored fully; variables are assigned undefined.)

Execution Phase:
Code runs line by line.

The greet() function is called → a new Execution Context is created for it.

It’s pushed onto the Call Stack.

Inside it, console.log("Hello Samad") runs.

When the function finishes → its context is popped out from the Call Stack.

When all code finishes, the Call Stack becomes empty again.

 Step 4: Event Loop (for async code)

If there’s something like:

console.log("Start");
setTimeout(() => console.log("Hello after 2s"), 2000);
console.log("End");


Execution:

“Start” → printed immediately.

setTimeout() → sent to the Web APIs (browser handles it separately).

“End” → printed.

After 2 seconds, the callback goes into the Callback Queue.

The Event Loop checks if the Call Stack is empty → then moves the callback to the stack → “Hello after 2s” prints.

 Summary
Concept	Description
Memory Heap	Stores variables & objects
Call Stack	Executes code line-by-line
Execution Context	Environment where code runs (Global or Function level)
Event Loop	Manages async operations
Web APIs / Callback Queue	Handle things like setTimeout, fetch, DOM events


